---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { formatUpdated, lastUpdated } from "../lib/updated";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const chapters = (await getCollection("chapters")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const updated = formatUpdated(lastUpdated("src/pages/index.astro"));
---

<Base title="himmel · WebGPU sky" description="Small, composable TypeScript + WebGPU libraries for rendering the sky.">
    <h1>himmel</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        A family of composable libraries for rendering the sky in real time with WebGPU.
    </p>
    <p id="about">
        The goal is a sky that is correct for any place, date and hour. Sun, Moon and stars in their true positions,
        the Moon with its phase and libration, solar and lunar eclipses, and physically based atmospheric scattering,
        down to the refraction that lifts a low Sun and the aerial perspective that fades distant geometry into haze.
        Clouds follow later.
    </p>
    <p>
        The same code precomputes what real-time rendering needs to stay fast: scattering look-up tables you can tune
        and recompute, and environment maps for image-based lighting that update as the Sun moves. Where a computed
        sky is not wanted, the classic image-based route stays open, with HDR sources and several projections. All of
        it is plain functions and compute-first WGSL, with no scene graph to adopt.
    </p>
    <p class="page-meta">Last updated {updated}.</p>
    <p class="status">
        <span class="beta">beta</span> Work in progress. The packages are usable but their APIs can still change.
    </p>
    <ul class="chapter-list">
        {
            chapters.map((c) => (
                <li>
                    <a href={`${base}/${c.id}/`}>
                        <span class="mono">{c.data.package}</span>
                        {c.data.planned && <span class="planned">planned</span>}
                        <p>{c.data.summary}</p>
                    </a>
                </li>
            ))
        }
    </ul>

    <p>
        It started with a book. Götz Hoeppe's{" "}
        <a href="https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691124537/why-the-sky-is-blue" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Why the Sky Is Blue</a> (German original:{" "}
        <a href="https://d-nb.info/956368794" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blau, die Farbe des Himmels</a>, 1999)
        follows two and a half thousand years of asking why the sky looks the way it does, from myth to atmospheric
        physics. It pulled me into the subject, and it is a good reminder of how much of what we understand began with
        people looking up and refusing to let the question go.
    </p>
    <p>
        Then came the work of{" "}
        <a href="http://www-evasion.imag.fr/Membres/Eric.Bruneton/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eric Bruneton</a> and
        Fabrice Neyret on precomputed atmospheric scattering. It was less a template than a challenge: I wanted to
        understand it well enough to take it apart and build my own and incorporate it into a real-time visualizations.
    </p>
    <p>
        Years later, three things came together. Maxime Heckel's{" "}
        <a href="https://blog.maximeheckel.com/posts/on-rendering-the-sky-sunsets-and-planets/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            On Rendering the Sky, Sunsets, and Planets</a> from May 2026 builds atmospheric scattering up from
        raymarching to look-up tables in WebGL, with a widget for every step. It is excellent, and proof that all of
        this now belongs in the browser. Agentic coding, part of my daily work by now, made a port of this size feel
        within reach. And then I watched the solar eclipse of 12 August 2026, and that settled it.
    </p>
    <p>
        So himmel is the successor of{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/cgcostume/osghimmel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">osgHimmel</a>, the C++ sky
        renderer behind my 2012{" "}
        <a
            href="https://daniellimberger.de/resources/2012%20%E2%80%93%20Mueller%20%28now%20Limberger%29%20%E2%80%93%20Photorealistisches%20Rendering%20atmosphaerischer%20Effekte%20in%20geovirtuellen%203D-Umgebungen%20in%20Echtzeit.pdf"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">master's thesis, Photorealistic Real-Time Rendering of Atmospheric Effects in Geovirtual
            3D Environments</a>, written in German, and the paper{" "}
        <a href="https://daniellimberger.de/publications.html#abstract-2012-vmv-sky-phenomena" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Single-Pass Rendering of Day and Night Sky Phenomena</a> (videos:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t8ibsEl0kAI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">computed sky</a>,{" "}
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y8UMaM_Wj-o" target="_blank" rel="noopener">texture mapped skies</a>).
        Everything from back then gets carried over, reviewed, tested and explained here, one chapter at a time, and
        modernized to compute-first WebGPU.
    </p>
    <p>
        The best way into all of this is still the oldest one: go outside and look up.
    </p>
</Base>
